---
// Homepage-only figure: the offline review path and the separate path that
// reads a live PostgreSQL catalog. Shared diagram styling lives in site.css.
const offline = [
  { x: 10, label: 'SQL files', note: 'and ordering' },
  { x: 240, label: 'Discovery', note: 'apply order' },
  { x: 470, label: 'Validation', note: 'headers, parse' },
  { x: 700, label: 'Dry-run review', note: 'shortened SQL' },
];
const target = [
  { x: 10, label: 'Live PostgreSQL', note: 'catalog' },
  { x: 300, label: 'Catalog drift', note: 'check' },
  { x: 560, label: 'Findings', note: 'for review' },
];
const offlineArrows = [180, 410, 640];
const targetArrows = [
  { from: 230, to: 300 },
  { from: 520, to: 560 },
];
---

<figure class="diagram migrator landing-path">
  <svg viewBox="0 0 900 300" role="img" aria-labelledby="path-title path-desc">
    <title id="path-title">Offline review path and target inspection path</title>
    <desc id="path-desc">
      Offline path: SQL files move through discovery, validation, then a dry-run
      review, with no database connection. Target inspection path: the live
      PostgreSQL catalog and the expected definitions in the SQL files are
      compared by a catalog drift check that reports findings for review. This
      diagram shows a review sequence, not an automatic execution.
    </desc>

    <text class="diagram-label" x="10" y="34">
      OFFLINE / NO TARGET
    </text>
    {offlineArrows.map((edge) => (
      <g class="diagram-arrow">
        <path
          d={`M${edge + 4} 95 H${edge + 44}`}
          fill="none"
          stroke-width="2"
        />
        <path
          d={`M${edge + 46} 89 L${edge + 54} 95 L${edge + 46} 101`}
          fill="none"
          stroke-width="2"
        />
      </g>
    ))}
    {offline.map((box) => (
      <g>
        <rect x={box.x} y="60" width="170" height="70" rx="8" />
        <g text-anchor="middle">
          <text x={box.x + 85} y="90">
            {box.label}
          </text>
          <text x={box.x + 85} y="112">
            {box.note}
          </text>
        </g>
      </g>
    ))}

    <path
      class="diagram-dash"
      d="M95 130 V170 H410 V204"
      fill="none"
      stroke-width="2"
    />
    <path
      class="diagram-dash"
      d="M403 196 L410 208 L417 196"
      fill="none"
      stroke-width="2"
    />
    <text class="diagram-label" x="250" y="162" text-anchor="middle">
      expected definitions
    </text>

    <text class="diagram-label" x="10" y="196">
      TARGET INSPECTION / CONNECTION REQUIRED
    </text>
    {targetArrows.map((arrow) => (
      <g class="diagram-arrow">
        <path
          d={`M${arrow.from + 4} 245 H${arrow.to - 14}`}
          fill="none"
          stroke-width="2"
        />
        <path
          d={`M${arrow.to - 12} 239 L${arrow.to - 4} 245 L${arrow.to - 12} 251`}
          fill="none"
          stroke-width="2"
        />
      </g>
    ))}
    {target.map((box) => (
      <g>
        <rect x={box.x} y="210" width="220" height="70" rx="8" />
        <g text-anchor="middle">
          <text x={box.x + 110} y="240">
            {box.label}
          </text>
          <text x={box.x + 110} y="262">
            {box.note}
          </text>
        </g>
      </g>
    ))}
  </svg>
  <figcaption>
    SQL files move through discovery, validation, and dry-run review without a
    target. Reading the live catalog is a separate path that needs a connection,
    and it reports differences instead of correcting them. The public
    demonstration executes no SQL.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
